## 2. Smooth sliding transitions across the entire application

### A. Main navigation transitions

Update `WorkspaceApp.tsx` and the existing routing/layout components.

Whenever a user clicks a sidebar item, transition smoothly into the selected section.

Apply consistent behavior to:

- Overview.
- Institution Registry.
- Complaint Cases.
- Overlap Detection.
- Bureau/Cell Coordination.
- Transfers & Escalations.
- Alerts & Notifications.
- Reports & Analytics.
- Audit Trail.
- Settings.

The sidebar and persistent application shell must remain stationary. Only the main content region should animate.

Use a subtle horizontal slide combined with a fade.

Recommended values:

- Duration: 180–240ms.
- Horizontal offset: 10–14px.
- Initial opacity: 0.
- Final opacity: 1.
- Easing: `cubic-bezier(0.22, 1, 0.36, 1)`.

The outgoing content should fade away with minimal movement. The incoming content should slide into its final position.

Do not use large left-to-right page sweeps, bouncing, zooming, or elastic movement.

### B. Apply the same animation to sub-items

Do not implement animations only for the primary sidebar.

Apply the same transition system to:

- Institution detail pages.
- Complaint detail pages.
- Related-case detail pages.
- Nested navigation routes.
- Dashboard drill-down views.
- Institution profile tabs.
- Case history tabs.
- Coordination detail views.
- Transfer detail views.
- Report categories.
- Settings subsections.
- Alert detail panels, when they replace the main content.
- Breadcrumb navigation between parent and child pages.

For example:

Complaint Cases → Select Case → Case Details → Transfer History.

Each navigation step should feel consistent and intentional.

When switching between sibling tabs, use a short fade or subtle slide. When navigating to a detail page, use the same small offset and easing as the other pages.

Avoid animating simple dropdown changes, checkbox toggles, filter updates, or unrelated state changes as though they were page navigation.

### C. One shared transition system

Implement a reusable page-transition component or integrate with the router's existing transition mechanism.

Use the project's current animation library if one already exists. Do not introduce another animation library unnecessarily.

Requirements:

- One consistent duration and easing system.
- Stable React keys based on the current route.
- No duplicate entrance animations.
- No competing CSS transitions on parent and child containers.
- No horizontal page overflow.
- No flashing or layout jumping.
- No re-animation of every chart and card on each render.
- No accidental animation when notification counts or other unrelated state changes.
- Preserve the correct active sidebar item during detail navigation.

If the project uses React Router, coordinate the transitions with the actual route changes instead of maintaining a second unrelated navigation state.

If the existing application uses conditional page rendering, refactor it into a stable route-aware system without breaking current features.

### D. Scroll restoration

Define predictable scroll behavior:

- Main navigation changes should start the new page at its expected scroll position.
- Returning to a previous detail page should restore its scroll position where appropriate.
- Switching tabs within the same detail page should not unexpectedly jump the user to the top.
- Opening a modal or notification popover should not reset page scroll.
- Animated content must not create horizontal scrolling.

Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`. When enabled, reduce the slide distance or replace the animation with a short fade.

